    {
      "paperId": "SCOPUS-PUBLICATION:84997610819",
      "doi": "",
      "title": "The definition of the low earth orbital environment and its effect on thermal control materials",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Durcanin J.T."
        },
        {
          "name": "Chalmers D.R."
        },
        {
          "name": "Visentine J.T."
        }
      ],
      "year": 1987,
      "abstract": "The most challenging space-based applications in the forseeable future are for low-Earth orbit (LEO) operations, specifically the Space Station program. the Space Station basically consists of a manned core and two co-orbiting platforms in \"meridian\" orbits, and a polar-orbiting platform. A primary design consideration, if unscheduled extra vehicle activity (SVA) repair or robot servicing tasks are to be minimised, is Che selection of suitable materials (especially for thermal control) with the stability to with stand long missions in LEO (20 to 30 years). Major constituents in the LEO environment that affect material properties are atomic oxygen, ionizing radiation (protons and electrons), solar (UV) radiation, and high-velocity solid particles. The definitions of these elements and their resultant effects, individually as well as synergistically, on the most commonly utilized thermal control materials, are reviewed and summarized in this paper.",
